"""Abstract, Results, Discussion, Conclusion. Every number below is copied from summary_full.txt / tables.json."""

ABSTRACT = ("Knowledge tracing (KT) models are usually compared with ranking metrics, yet adaptive systems act on predicted probabilities, "
            "and the quality of those probabilities may deteriorate when response histories are short or contain errors. This study audits the "
            "next-response probabilities of five predictors, a smoothed item-accuracy baseline, Bayesian knowledge tracing with forgetting, "
            "2PL item response theory (2PL-IRT), a recurrent deep KT model, and a simplified attention-based model, under "
            "controlled sparsity and noise. Learners were simulated from two generators with different assumptions (two-state guess–slip and dynamic item response) "
            ", in five replicates of 3,000 learners, with visible histories of "
            "3 to 50 events, history label flips of 0–20%, and varied guessing and slipping. All results come from the executed code; no real data were analysed. "
            "We report ROC-AUC, Brier score, negative log-likelihood, expected calibration error, and high-confidence errors with learner-level bootstrap "
            "intervals, and use temperature scaling as a post-hoc reference. Shorter histories worsened Brier score and calibration for most models, but model "
            "ordering depended on the generator: under the dynamic generator no model beat the item baseline on Brier score at three visible events, and under the two-state "
            "generator 2PL-IRT discrimination fell as history grew. Label flips lowered discrimination and, with short histories, removed the advantage over the baseline "
            "in several settings. High-confidence predictions were rare unless slipping was low. Temperature scaling changed little at long histories and helped more when refitted "
            "at short histories, whereas a temperature fitted on clean long histories transferred poorly. These simulation findings concern evaluation practice, not real classrooms.")

