DISCUSSION = [
    ("h1", "4. Discussion"),
    ("p", "Three results seem to us the most useful, and none of them says which model is best. The first is that the ranking reverses with the generator. Under KT-B no model beat the smoothed item baseline on Brier score at three visible events, and under KT-A 2PL-IRT stayed close to it once histories were long. A comparison built on one generating process, or one history length, could be made to support almost any ordering. The second is that discrimination and calibration came apart. At three visible events under KT-A, BKT, DKT, and the AKT-style model were clearly ahead of the baseline in AUC and Brier score and still had higher ECE; a report that stopped at AUC would have missed this. The third is practical: a temperature fitted on clean long histories did not carry over to short ones, because the fitted value moved with history length and noise level."),
    ("p", "These results point the same way as earlier findings that deep models are not uniformly better than carefully specified classical ones (Gervet et al., 2020; Khajah et al., 2016) and that calibration can degrade under shift (Ovadia et al., 2019), and they bear on recent work on uncertainty in KT (Cheng et al., 2025; Christie et al., 2024; Mitton et al., 2026). They do not show that these effects occur in real learner data, and we make no claim about which family will be better calibrated there. What we would take from them is a modest evaluation habit. When probabilities will be acted on, report calibration and Brier score beside AUC, keep a simple baseline in the comparison, evaluate at several history lengths, and say whether any recalibration was fitted in the deployment environment or carried over from another."),
    ("p", "The limits are substantial. Everything is simulated, and the two generators are ours. They contain no multi-skill items, no cold-start items, no timing or help requests, and no non-random missingness, and each favours some models, so the ordering is a property of this experiment and should not be extrapolated. The AKT-style model is simplified and the networks are small. Hyperparameters were chosen once, on a separate development replicate with clean full-length histories, which may have disadvantaged the short-history conditions. Five replicates are few, and we ran no significance tests. Flipped labels are symmetric and random, whereas real record errors are probably systematic. The high-confidence analysis rests on small counts, ECE is biased and depends on binning, and a single temperature cannot repair a shift in the mean prediction. Guessing and slipping were varied only at generation, so matched and test-only effects could not be separated for them."),
    ("p", "The obvious next step is to repeat the protocol on public logs such as ASSISTments (Feng et al., 2009) and EdNet (Choi et al., 2020), after checking versions, licences, and fields, and to add richer calibration maps, abstention rules, and models with explicit uncertainty. Work with particular learner groups or classrooms would need separate designs and, where learners are involved, ethical review. This paper offers no evidence on teaching efficiency, student retention, special education practice, or individualised education plans."),
]

CONCLUSION = [
    ("h1", "5. Conclusion"),
    ("p", "We asked how short and noisy histories affect the probabilities that five next-response predictors assign, using simulated learners from two different generators. For RQ1, shorter histories lowered AUC and raised Brier score and ECE for most models, but the size, and sometimes the direction, of the effect depended on model and generator; under one generator no model improved on a smoothed item baseline in Brier score at three visible events. For RQ2, flipped history labels reduced discrimination and, together with short histories, removed the advantage over the baseline in several settings. High-confidence predictions were rare except at low slip, and where they occurred the error proportions were in several cases above the 0.10 expected of a calibrated predictor, on small counts. For RQ3, temperature scaling changed little when histories were long, helped more when refitted for short histories under one generator, and transferred poorly from a clean long-history reference."),
    ("p", "These conclusions hold for the simulated settings, these implementations, and these metrics. They do not establish performance on real learners and say nothing about educational outcomes. What the paper contributes is a reproducible protocol for reporting discrimination, calibration, and high-confidence errors under sparse and noisy histories; replication on real data is the next step."),
]
